> There's a cast to QString because the LHS, `yaml_text` in that instance (and similarly in the other places) is a QString.

The LHS is a `QString`.

The RHS is a `QByteArray`.

The operator is `+=`.

At least in 5.12.12, there is a

```
inline QT_ASCII_CAST_WARN QString &operator+=(const QByteArray &s)
    { return append(QString::fromUtf8(s)); }
```

method, which is presumably what should be used if you have a `+=` operator with a `QString` LHS and a `QByteArray` RHS, and there is a

```
static inline QString fromUtf8(const QByteArray &str)
    { return str.isNull() ? QString() : fromUtf8(str.data(), qstrnlen(str.constData(), str.size())); }
```

method, which is presumably what should be used if you are doing `QString::fromUtf8(s)` when `s` is a `QByteArray`.

So where is the requirement for a cast here?

> The version of Qt is 5.15.3, but the code in qstring.h is the same, including eventually calling this:
> 
> ```cpp
>     static inline QString fromUtf8(const char *str, int size = -1)
>     {
>         return fromUtf8_helper(str, (str && size == -1) ? int(strlen(str)) : size);
>     }
> ```

That should be used only if you do `QString::fromUtf8(str)` where `str` is a `const char *`.  The only `char *`s I see in

    yaml_text += "      " + base64_data.toBase64() + "\n";`

are `"      "` and `"\n"` - but those are used with `+` operators where the other operand is a `QByteArray`.  There is a

```
inline const QByteArray operator+(const char *a1, const QByteArray &a2)
{ return QByteArray(a1) += a2; }
```

method and a

```
inline const QByteArray operator+(const QByteArray &a1, const char *a2)
{ return QByteArray(a1) += a2; }
```

method, which is what should be used for the two `+` operators in the RHS.  The first of them directly constructs a new `QByteArray` from the LHS of `+`, using this constructor:

```
QByteArray::QByteArray(const char *data, int size)
{
    if (!data) {
        d = Data::sharedNull();
    } else {
        if (size < 0)
            size = int(strlen(data));
        if (!size) {
            d = Data::allocate(0);
        } else {
            d = Data::allocate(uint(size) + 1u);
            Q_CHECK_PTR(d);
            d->size = size;   
            memcpy(d->data(), data, size);
            d->data()[size] = '\0';
        }
    }
}
```

which is declared as `QByteArray(const char *, int size = -1);`, so `size` would be -1.  There's no `fromUtf8` involved there.

The second of them appends creates a new `QByteArray` from the LHS, which is a `QByteArray` (i.e., making a separate `QByteArray` so that we don't append to the LHS) and appends the RHS to it; the RHS is a `const char *` string, so that presumably uses

```
inline QByteArray &QByteArray::operator+=(const char *s)
{ return append(s); }
```

and the `append` method would presumably be

```
QByteArray& QByteArray::append(const char *str)
{
    if (str) {
        const int len = int(strlen(str));
        if (d->ref.isShared() || uint(d->size + len) + 1u > d->alloc)
            reallocData(uint(d->size + len) + 1u, d->detachFlags() | Data::Grow);
        memcpy(d->data() + d->size, str, len + 1); // include null terminator
        d->size += len;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

so, again, no `fromUtf8`.

So I see nothing that calls `QString::fromUtf8` on a `const char *` on the RHS.

The RHS evaluates to a `QByteArray`, so

    yaml_text += "      " + base64_data.toBase64() + "\n";`

is `{a QString} += {a QByteArray}`, thus the `+=` method that should be used would be

```
    inline QT_ASCII_CAST_WARN QString &operator+=(const QByteArray &s)
    { return append(QString::fromUtf8(s)); }
```

but that's calling `fromUtf8` on a `QByteArray`, not on a `const char *`, so it should use the

```
    static inline QString fromUtf8(const QByteArray &str)
    { return str.isNull() ? QString() : fromUtf8(str.data(), qstrnlen(str.constData(), str.size())); }
```

method.  In this particular case, `str.isNull()` will presumably return `false`, as the RHS didn't just pop out of a constructor with no argument or a null-pointer argument, so it should call `fromUtf8` - but with a count derived from `qstrnlen()`, *not* `strlen()`.  So. in 

```cpp
    static inline QString fromUtf8(const char *str, int size = -1)
    {
        return fromUtf8_helper(str, (str && size == -1) ? int(strlen(str)) : size);
    }
```

`size` would only by -1 if, somehow, `qstrnlen()` happened to return -1.  The `qstrnlen()` is defined in qbytearray.h as

```
inline uint qstrnlen(const char *str, uint maxlen)
{
    uint length = 0;
    if (str) {
        while (length < maxlen && *str++)
            length++;
    }
    return length; 
}  
```

which returns a `uint` and thus could only produce -1 if its result were 4294967295 - is *that* prospect what's driving GCC into a paroxysm of terror?

Hmm, trying a few variants:

```c++
QByteArray base64_encoded = "       " + base64_data.toBase64() + "\n";
yaml_text += QString::fromUtf8(base64_encoded, qstrlen(base64_encoded));
```

does not have the warning, but 

```c++
QByteArray base64_encoded = "       " + base64_data.toBase64() + "\n";
yaml_text += QString::fromUtf8(base64_encoded, qstrnlen(base64_encoded, base64_encoded.size()));
```

*does* have the same warning.

Even more bizarrely to me, in `about_dialog.cpp` I can do this:
```c++
QByteArray qt_ba = gstring_free_to_qbytearray(get_compiled_version_info(gather_wireshark_qt_compiled_info)) + "\n";
int size = -1;
if (!qt_ba.isNull())
    size = int(qstrlen(qt_ba));
clipboardInfo += QString::fromUtf8(qt_ba, size);
```

and get the warning *in `qstrlen`*:
```c++
In file included from /usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qvariant.h:44,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qabstractitemmodel.h:43,
