```

whereas *not* checking `qt.isNull()` is fine and produces no warning:
```c++
QByteArray qt_ba = gstring_free_to_qbytearray(get_compiled_version_info(gather_wireshark_qt_compiled_info)) + "\n";
int size = -1;
//if (!qt_ba.isNull())
    size = int(qstrlen(qt_ba));
clipboardInfo += QString::fromUtf8(qt_ba, size);
```

but if I use `qstrnlen` instead of `qstrlen` I don't get the warning if I check for null:
```c++
QByteArray qt_ba = gstring_free_to_qbytearray(get_compiled_version_info(gather_wireshark_qt_compiled_info)) + "\n";
int size = -1;
if (!qt_ba.isNull())
    size = int(qstrnlen(qt_ba, qt_ba.size()));
clipboardInfo += QString::fromUtf8(qt_ba, size);
```

but I *do* get the warning if I don't check for null:
```c++
QByteArray qt_ba = gstring_free_to_qbytearray(get_compiled_version_info(gather_wireshark_qt_compiled_info)) + "\n";
int size = -1;
//if (!qt_ba.isNull())
    size = int(qstrnlen(qt_ba, qt_ba.size()));
clipboardInfo += QString::fromUtf8(qt_ba, size);
```

I get the same results for using `isEmpty()` instead of `isNull()`.

Since the [Qt documentation](https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qbytearray.html#details) for `QByteArray` claims that "Behind the scenes, it always ensures that the data is followed by a '\0' terminator" and "the byte array also maintains an extra '\0' character at the end so that if a function is used that asks for a pointer to the underlying data (e.g. a call to data()), the data pointed to is guaranteed to be '\0'-terminated," I am confused by the difference.

The most bizarre error by far is in `wireshark_main_window_slots.cpp` where the original code:

```c++
if (filter_ba.isEmpty()) {
    filter_ba = interface_opts->cfilter;
} else {
    /* Not the first selected interface; is its capture filter
       the same as the one the other interfaces we've looked
       at have? */
    if (strcmp(interface_opts->cfilter, filter_ba.constData()) != 0) {
        /* No, so not all selected interfaces have the same capture
           filter. */
        filter_ba.clear();
    }
}
```

has this error:
```c++
/home/johnthacker/wireshark/ui/qt/wireshark_main_window_slots.cpp:953:31: error: ‘int strcmp(const char*, const char*)’ reading 1 or more bytes from a region of size 0 [-Werror=stringop-overread]
  953 |                     if (strcmp(interface_opts->cfilter, filter_ba.constData()) != 0) {
      |                         ~~~~~~^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
In file included from /usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qbytearray.h:46,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qstring.h:50,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qobject.h:47,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtWidgets/qwidget.h:45,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtWidgets/qmainwindow.h:44,
                 from /usr/include/qt5/QtWidgets/QMainWindow:1,
                 from /home/johnthacker/wireshark/ui/qt/wireshark_main_window.h:58,
                 from /home/johnthacker/wireshark/ui/qt/wireshark_main_window_slots.cpp:18:
/usr/include/qt5/QtCore/qarraydata.h:129:8: note: at offset 24 into source object ‘QTypedArrayData<char>::<anonymous>’ of size 24
  129 | struct QTypedArrayData
      |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
```

but adding an additional check for `isNull()` removes the error:
```diff
-                if (filter_ba.isEmpty()) {
+                if (filter_ba.isEmpty() || filter_ba.isNull()) {
                     filter_ba = interface_opts->cfilter;
                 } else {
                     /* Not the first selected interface; is its capture filter
                       the same as the one the other interfaces we've looked
                       at have? */
                    if (strcmp(interface_opts->cfilter, filter_ba.constData()) != 0) {
```

despite the documentation claiming that "A null byte array is always empty, but an empty byte array isn't necessarily null." In addition, *only* checking `isNull()` and even having *no* `isNull()` or `isEmpty()` also has no warning, and it's ridiculous that calling `strcmp()` in *more* situations would avoid the warning.

There seems to be a trip-up between QByteArray and QString if QByteArray is being explicitly used.

But, at least in my very preliminary testing, it can be avoided, by using QString::fromUtf8 which accepts a character array as an input:

```
int len = current_pos + base64_raw_len < nchars ? base64_raw_len : (int) nchars - current_pos;
QString content = QString::fromUtf8(&buffer[current_pos], len);
yaml_text += "      " + content.toUtf8().toBase64() + "\n";
```

The second part again has a QByteArray inbetween (as output for toUtf8()), but in my case it works. But I agree with @guyharris the issue shouldn't be one in the first place. Seems the compiler gets tripped up by Qts internal tricks.

